{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_115","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"uv run python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('agent/report.md');s=p.read_text();start=s.index('Tests of a global');end=s.index('## Results');s=s[:start]+'''A global 15% width reduction failed, as did a finer seven-day seasonal profile and a fitted range/volume residual correction. The residual model improved a future-volatility proxy but reversed sign between September and October on actual CRPS. This distinction matters: predicting volatility more accurately under a surrogate loss does not necessarily improve the submitted distribution.\n\nLognormal path-level volatility uncertainty (log standard deviation 0.35, unit second moment) improves one-hour crypto and commodity/equity forecasts. It worsens crypto 24-hour forecasts, where we retain Gaussian increments. The selected mixture improves all 39 affected stress-test cells. Minute-of-hour profiles add a smaller consistent crypto gain: 19/20 initial cells improve. Sobol sampling has small, seed-dependent effects and is not selected universally.\n\nCalendar kernels use June–August events as independent observations, pooling assets within each release and shrinking estimates. Simultaneous releases combine by maximum rather than multiplication. Prospective tests cover later CPI/PPI, FOMC and payroll windows: 49/54 cells improve, with all nine configuration/window averages favorable. Holiday suppression is not selected. Stronger kernels and a newer commodity/equity seasonal profile are undergoing incremental combined-model validation.\n\nA small public pretrained model, Chronos-T5-tiny, forecasts hourly deseasonalized variance from context alone. A quarter-blend improves 9/10 initial one-hour cells but slightly worsens crypto 24-hour results. It remains experimental pending comparison against the stronger fast-memory baseline. Offline weights, exact revision, latency and reproducibility checks are recorded in the journal.\n\nHistorical validation uses the official backtester scoring worker on cached validator prompt times. It exactly reproduces ordinary backtest CRPS on three representative parity checks. With profiles fitted only through July1, seasonality improves 33/36 July/August asset-window cells and all six configuration means. These runs make no field-rank claim.\n\n'''+s[end:];start=s.index('These are official mean');end=s.index('On the available CPU');s=s[:start]+'''These are official CRPS means, averaged equally across assets within each configuration, at 1000 paths. Candidate v1 combines seasonal EWMA, crypto within-hour profiles, calendar kernels and the group-specific volatility mixture. It improves all 36 asset/format/window cells in the two ten-day comparisons.\n\n| Scored days | Configuration | Starter | Candidate v1 |\n|---|---|---:|---:|\n| Sep09–18 | CRYPTO_24H | 4296.6726 | 4217.6681 |\n| Sep09–18 | COM_EQU_24H | 2316.0261 | 2101.4462 |\n| Sep09–18 | CRYPTO_1H | 960.4265 | 927.0646 |\n| Oct01–10 | CRYPTO_24H | 3531.2042 | 3426.4855 |\n| Oct01–10 | COM_EQU_24H | 2065.4112 | 1818.4707 |\n| Oct01–10 | CRYPTO_1H | 741.2317 | 691.7005 |\n\nAll 20 daily configuration means improve, although individual asset-days can regress (HYPE one-hour on Sep17 is one example). Mean per-asset percentage reductions are 2.00%, 8.74%, 3.77% in September and 3.07%, 11.84%, 6.91% in October, in table configuration order. Ratios of the aggregate means differ because those give greater influence to assets with larger raw CRPS. These are development-inclusive validation windows, not untouched holdouts or the unseen campaign score.\n\n'''+s[end:];s=s.replace('The initial evaluation covers four scored days, so the apparent consistency is encouraging but not a full regime test.','Validation includes two ten-day windows, release stress windows, and earlier causal-profile checks, but substantial repeated model selection remains.');start=s.index('## What more budget is for');s=s[:start]+'''## What more budget is for\n\nFinish incremental checks of faster volatility memory, calendar strength and profile refresh; test filtered empirical innovations; check canonical float32 output precision and multiple seeds of the final combination. These studies remain active. Final selection will follow official paired CRPS and serving reproducibility.\n''';p.write_text(s)\nPY\nuv run agent/summarize.py eia_exclusion --base stress_event_power; tail -3 agent/results/innovation_diagnostics.log; cat agent/BUDGET.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_115","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"uv run python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('agent/report.md');s=p.read_text();start=s.index('Tests of a global');end=s.index('## Results');s=s[:start]+'''A global 15% width reduction failed, as did a finer seven-day seasonal profile and a fitted range/volume residual correction. The residual model improved a future-volatility proxy but reversed sign between September and October on actual CRPS. This distinction matters: predicting volatility more accurately under a surrogate loss does not necessarily improve the submitted distribution.\n\nLognormal path-level volatility uncertainty (log standard deviation 0.35, unit second moment) improves one-hour crypto and commodity/equity forecasts. It worsens crypto 24-hour forecasts, where we retain Gaussian increments. The selected mixture improves all 39 affected stress-test cells. Minute-of-hour profiles add a smaller consistent crypto gain: 19/20 initial cells improve. Sobol sampling has small, seed-dependent effects and is not selected universally.\n\nCalendar kernels use June–August events as independent observations, pooling assets within each release and shrinking estimates. Simultaneous releases combine by maximum rather than multiplication. Prospective tests cover later CPI/PPI, FOMC and payroll windows: 49/54 cells improve, with all nine configuration/window averages favorable. Holiday suppression is not selected. Stronger kernels and a newer commodity/equity seasonal profile are undergoing incremental combined-model validation.\n\nA small public pretrained model, Chronos-T5-tiny, forecasts hourly deseasonalized variance from context alone. A quarter-blend improves 9/10 initial one-hour cells but slightly worsens crypto 24-hour results. It remains experimental pending comparison against the stronger fast-memory baseline. Offline weights, exact revision, latency and reproducibility checks are recorded in the journal.\n\nHistorical validation uses the official backtester scoring worker on cached validator prompt times. It exactly reproduces ordinary backtest CRPS on three representative parity checks. With profiles fitted only through July1, seasonality improves 33/36 July/August asset-window cells and all six configuration means. These runs make no field-rank claim.\n\n'''+s[end:];start=s.index('These are official mean');end=s.index('On the available CPU');s=s[:start]+'''These are official CRPS means, averaged equally across assets within each configuration, at 1000 paths. Candidate v1 combines seasonal EWMA, crypto within-hour profiles, calendar kernels and the group-specific volatility mixture. It improves all 36 asset/format/window cells in the two ten-day comparisons.\n\n| Scored days | Configuration | Starter | Candidate v1 |\n|---|---|---:|---:|\n| Sep09–18 | CRYPTO_24H | 4296.6726 | 4217.6681 |\n| Sep09–18 | COM_EQU_24H | 2316.0261 | 2101.4462 |\n| Sep09–18 | CRYPTO_1H | 960.4265 | 927.0646 |\n| Oct01–10 | CRYPTO_24H | 3531.2042 | 3426.4855 |\n| Oct01–10 | COM_EQU_24H | 2065.4112 | 1818.4707 |\n| Oct01–10 | CRYPTO_1H | 741.2317 | 691.7005 |\n\nAll 20 daily configuration means improve, although individual asset-days can regress (HYPE one-hour on Sep17 is one example). Mean per-asset percentage reductions are 2.00%, 8.74%, 3.77% in September and 3.07%, 11.84%, 6.91% in October, in table configuration order. Ratios of the aggregate means differ because those give greater influence to assets with larger raw CRPS. These are development-inclusive validation windows, not untouched holdouts or the unseen campaign score.\n\n'''+s[end:];s=s.replace('The initial evaluation covers four scored days, so the apparent consistency is encouraging but not a full regime test.','Validation includes two ten-day windows, release stress windows, and earlier causal-profile checks, but substantial repeated model selection remains.');start=s.index('## What more budget is for');s=s[:start]+'''## What more budget is for\n\nFinish incremental checks of faster volatility memory, calendar strength and profile refresh; test filtered empirical innovations; check canonical float32 output precision and multiple seeds of the final combination. These studies remain active. Final selection will follow official paired CRPS and serving reproducibility.\n''';p.write_text(s)\nPY\nuv run agent/summarize.py eia_exclusion --base stress_event_power; tail -3 agent/results/innovation_diagnostics.log; cat agent/BUDGET.md\"","aggregated_output":"\n eia_exclusion cells 3\n                             crps       base     pct  wins  cells\nend        config                                                \n2026-09-13 COM_EQU_24H  4260.5523  4260.4122  0.0033     0      1\n2026-09-19 COM_EQU_24H  3578.6706  3578.6895 -0.0005     1      1\n2026-10-05 COM_EQU_24H  3004.9325  3004.9325  0.0000     0      1\nend         2026-09-13  2026-09-19  2026-10-05\nasset  fmt                                    \nWTIOIL 24h         0.0        -0.0         0.0\nWTIOIL {1: {'n': 119978, 'kurtosis': 43.72405003165474, 'tail3': 0.011418760106019436, 'abs_quantiles': [0.47946289911279105, 1.4921083883319521, 3.107408654825897]}, 5: {'n': 119877, 'kurtosis': 48.254900158027795, 'tail3': 0.010026944284558338, 'abs_quantiles': [0.5109298102411359, 1.4728038884830845, 3.00144747136557]}}\nSPCX {1: {'n': 120738, 'kurtosis': 170.07703478975458, 'tail3': 0.012696913979028971, 'abs_quantiles': [0.4401492993557512, 1.4645248918660743, 3.2048037032154912]}, 5: {'n': 120686, 'kurtosis': 151.25776855733582, 'tail3': 0.01088775831496611, 'abs_quantiles': [0.4639711874260847, 1.4709144547022908, 3.0616570310524702]}}\nSP500 {1: {'n': 120553, 'kurtosis': 24.578749774256256, 'tail3': 0.013545909268122734, 'abs_quantiles': [0.4400325564325551, 1.5064010318288827, 3.275499088770933]}, 5: {'n': 120486, 'kurtosis': 9.558120420155303, 'tail3': 0.012142489583852065, 'abs_quantiles': [0.52148634833951, 1.575042869