{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_301","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n '0.25|\\\\.25|0.45|\\\\.45|mixture|mix' agent/journal.md | head -28; head -60 agent/fit_seasonality.py; cat agent/results/queue_median_full.log; du -sh agent/results; git diff --stat -- agent/modeling.py\"","aggregated_output":"21:Change: research_model estimates 1-minute variance for 1h and 5-minute variance for 24h, with fast/slow EWMA mixtures and forecast mean reversion. No interpolation. Fit weekday/weekend half-hour profiles on June–August only, using local New York time, normalized rolling variance and a blend of squared/absolute returns with shrinkage. Test flat EWMA and then seasonal EWMA separately at 1000 paths.\n73:Iteration 2 result: path volatility mixture improves all 10 crypto1h asset-window cells; equal-asset relative gains are 0.293% in September and 0.535% in October versus seasonal Gaussian. All four daily group means improve, although individual asset-days can regress. Student-t is weaker (0.168% and 0.332%). For crypto24h both tails models slightly worsen the equal-asset means (mixture +0.098%/+0.019%, Student +0.121%/+0.012%). Verdict: KEEP mixture as a candidate for crypto1h, DISCARD blanket heavy tails for crypto24h. Com/equ mixture and Student both look favorable but need broader validation. Full numbers in results/tails_comparison.txt and raw paired artifacts.\n77:Iteration 2 follow-up: test the mixture on the new stress windows, retaining Gaussian innovations for crypto24h. The first selection is based on horizon/group behavior, not per-asset cherry-picking. The full candidate still needs an independent-seed comparison later.\n87:Checkpoint (campaign still active, no CHAMPION): baseline/ewma/seasonal/mixture/student/scale85 initial suites completed. Provisional serving modeling.py is seasonal Gaussian and independent of MODEL_CONFIG. Research knobs live only in research_model.py. Current running suites: stress_seasonal and stress_events (4 workers each), weekly and clock (2 each), stress_mixture (2). Total cap 14 workers. All start from workspace root, use uv run, exact official scoring, 1000 paths. Failed/partial Sep09 coverage artifacts moved to results/coverage_failed to prevent accidental inclusion in comparisons. First Sep13 event window improves 17/18 cells, but requires later-event confirmation. Read agent/BUDGET.md on every iteration; last status ~$3.79/100 and 17.8h remaining, no landing order.\n89:Next work: finish pending suites; validate horizon-specific mixture + calendar combination; test optional finite-ensemble samplers across seeds; assess shorter/longer volatility memory only after seasonal baseline. Broaden to 10-day evaluation before nomination. Reports are drafts and must be updated. Every deliverable including a real-commit CHAMPION remains required at landing; do not nominate now because promising experiments remain. The active goal tracks campaign continuation.\n111:Resource scheduling: on this resumed process tree, bounded queues reserve the cores of existing suites instead of oversubscribing. After clock exits: residual proxy fit, fine-event diagnostics/fit, then Sobol suite (2 cores). After stress_events exits: aligned-event suite (4 cores). After stress_seasonal exits: seeded starter baseline on TWO TEN-DAY windows, scored Sep09–18 and Oct01–10 inclusive (4 cores). Current weekly and stress_mixture retain 2 cores each. Maximum remains 14. New baseline_full establishes the final longer-window comparison; it does not use ranks or post-cutoff data. Queues are scripts, not agents; no subagent delegation.\n117:Iteration 5 completed: minute-of-hour profile wins 19/20 crypto cells. Equal-asset gains: crypto24h 0.0622%/0.0704%, crypto1h 0.0821%/0.1449% in September/October. Verdict: KEEP for combined validation. Seven-day shape: crypto1h worsens in both windows (0.1564%,0.0367%); October crypto24h worsens all five assets, averaging 0.2606%; com/equ signs mix. Verdict: DISCARD global day-specific refinement. Lesson: more seasonal parameters can add noise even when a coarser temporal shape is strongly useful.\n123:Hypothesis: a small pretrained time-series model can forecast the slowly evolving residual volatility level beyond a fixed EWMA mixture. Use Chronos-T5-tiny on hourly deseasonalized realized volatility, not on raw price levels. Public Hugging Face model metadata identifies revision 29d808298f1a62493e7b9a5e08529d0d930fa189, created 2024-02-28, last modified 2025-11-21 (before all validation dates). No online prices will be fetched. Commit any selected weights; production remains offline.\n137:Iteration 2 stress confirmation: mixture0.35 improves all 39 affected group/asset/window cells (24 com/equ and 15 crypto1h); crypto24h is intentionally unchanged. Com/equ gains are 0.650%,0.904%,2.192% on Sep13,Sep19,Oct05; crypto1h gains 0.951%,0.945%,1.432%. Verdict: KEEP horizon/group-specific mixture for combined evaluation. This is stronger evidence than the initial weekday-only gains.\n141:## Iteration 11 — shrinkage in quiet sessions; mixture strength\n145:Hypothesis B: the strong mixture gains during quieter stress windows may favor larger uncertainty. Separately test log-volatility std 0.50 versus the measured 0.35, with unchanged seasonal table. Evaluate the full initial grid to document the crypto24h tradeoff instead of assuming a group-specific improvement.\n153:Hypothesis: separately validated seasonal, within-hour, event and group-specific mixture effects survive combination. Candidate v1 combines original seasonal EWMA, crypto within-hour shape, five-minute event kernels, and mixture0.35 for crypto1h and com/equ24h only. No unvalidated holiday suppression, residual ML, refresh, stronger mixture, finer kernel, or sampling change is included.\n167:Iteration 11 initial result: 2% seasonal shrinkage improves all ten crypto24h cells (means -0.0404%/-0.0482%), but com/equ and crypto1h signs reverse between windows. Keep only as a candidate needing stress tests. Mixture0.50 is weaker and less consistent than 0.35 on initial windows; crypto24h worsens every cell (+0.602%/+0.438%). Retain 0.35 as the preferred mixture. No parameter is selected from a favorable asset alone.\n171:Hypothesis: the remaining one-hour error may come from the fixed 60-minute fast EWMA. Test 15-minute and 240-minute fast half-lives separately, keeping the six-hour/three-day mixture weights, forecast reversion, seasonal shape and Gaussian innovations unchanged. This isolates memory from the learned residual layer that failed cross-window validation.\n173:Change: two 1000-path crypto1h suites on the original matched windows. Kill criterion: require mean improvement on both windows and most assets before combining with the validated volatility mixture.\n224:Iteration 18 diagnostic reveals strong residual tails (e.g. XAU minute kurtosis32, >3-sigma probability1.49%; Gaussian benchmarks3 and0.27%). Build symmetric per-asset innovation quantiles from June–August standardized returns, cap absolute standardized shocks at8 before quantization to limit unstable extreme samples, then normalize the finite quantile grid to unit variance. Evaluate empirical innovations alone against the matched seasonal Gaussian; do not combine with path mixtures until this isolated test passes. Quantile clipping and fitting choices are fixed before CRPS results.\n230:Iteration 14 full combined result: candidate_v2 improves all ten full-window crypto1h asset cells relative to v1. Equal-asset CRPS September 927.0646→925.4813, October 691.7005→691.3552; mean per-asset gains0.1456%/0.0585%. KEEP the 15-minute fast half-life for the next serving candidate. This gain survives the selected path-level volatility mixture.\n238:## Iteration 20 — paired antithetic sampling with volatility mixtures\n240:Hypothesis: pairing each complete innovation vector with its negative reduces finite-ensemble location error. For a volatility mixture, each pair must share the same volatility draw; otherwise the intended symmetry is lost. Implement this pairing only behind a research option, preserving Gaussian/mixture distributions and environment-derived seeds. Compare candidate v1 against paired antithetic on BTC/SOL/AAPLX/SP500, both formats where applicable, two initial windows, three seeds (20261011,11,37). This is a sampling change, not extra market information. Require favorable pooled mean in each configuration and consistency across seeds before promotion; report any one-hour volatility-score tradeoff. Do not select based on one seed.\n260:Iteration18 initial result: empirical innovations improve crypto1h9/10 cells versus Gaussian (mean gains0.1570%/0.3407%), but lose all10 against the selected path mixture (+0.1369%/+0.1952%). COM improves10/16 versus Gaussian (-0.4895%/-0.2302%) but signs reverse versus mixture (-0.0176%/+0.0549%). Crypto24h worsens both window means (+0.1773%/+0.0826%). DISCARD empirical innovations as a replacement for the selected distributions. One distinct follow-up remains warranted: independent-increment tails could complement path-level uncertainty. Test empirical+mixture0.35 against mixture0.35 for COM and crypto1h only; no crypto24h promotion. Reject if either window regresses for a group.\n264:Iteration9 next combined test registered: add Chronos quarter-blend to candidate_v3 for crypto1h on both full ten-day windows. Compare against candidate_v2_full (v3 is exactly identical for1h), holding fast15, clock, event kernels and mixture0.35 fixed. Require favorable means in both windows and predominantly favorable asset cells. Run only after the existing GPU suite exits; one GPU worker throughout.\n272:Empirical-mixture scheduling correction: initial command unintentionally included redundant crypto24h cells. Stopped that suite, added an explicit skip-crypto24h option, archived the already generated redundant24h artifacts, and restarted only the26 affected cells. No redundant output enters the planned group comparisons.\n278:Hypothesis: the selected mixture gives each path one constant latent volatility for its whole horizon. A stationary mean-reverting log-volatility process can preserve every base-step mixture marginal while allowing uncertainty to change over time. Replace the constant latent normal with an AR(1) Gaussian state, half-life one quarter of the forecast horizon (15min for1h,6h for24h), same stationary std0.35 and unit expected variance multiplier. Preserve the original initial latent draw and innovation draws for a closer comparison. No return drift or cross-asset inputs are introduced.\n280:Test against mixture0.35 at1000 paths on initial windows for COM and crypto1h. Kill criterion: improve both configuration/window means and most asset cells before stress/combination. The empirical innovation experiment already illustrates that more within-path variation can harm one-hour volatility CRPS; report that tradeoff rather than assuming stochastic volatility must win. Queue behind empirical_mix's two workers.\n290:Iteration18 combined result: empirical+mixture loses all10 crypto1h cells (+0.2297%/+0.1081%) and both COM means (+0.0448%/+0.2643%). DISCARD empirical innovation layer entirely for selected serving. Lesson: extra tails can double-count uncertainty already captured by a path mixture.\n292:Iteration21 stress result: robust COM variance improves20/24 cells, all three means (-0.2094%/-0.2407%/-0.6176%). KEEP for incremental full-window comparison on candidate_v3, which includes calendar, mixture and refreshed COM profile. Compare September to v1 and October to candidate_refresh (exactly the corresponding v3 COM behavior). Schedule this3-worker suite after candidate_events releases its3 workers. Do not yet promote robust variance: stress gains versus Gaussian can disappear with the selected mixture.\n302:Exploratory zero-mass diagnostic: fitted empirical quantiles show only0.4–4.8% exact zero5-minute returns for most assets (XAU11.1%). Minute rates are larger for SOL/XRP/XAU. Given the empirical-tail layer's failure with the selected mixture and small24h zero mass, do not add a separate zero-inflation layer now.\n308:Hypothesis: the path-mixture improvement is concentrated in the one-hour volatility score, whereas empirical or time-varying innovations can worsen it despite more realistic individual returns. Retain the official scorer's already-computed detailed return values through a pass-through recorder inside the cached backtester path; do not reproduce or alter any scoring formula. Record interval totals, especially Vol and Overall, in prompt parquet. Gate the new diagnostics on exact total-score parity with a completed normal run.\n\"\"\"Estimate recurring variance shape using only prices before the specified training end.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom synth_lib.preparation.config import default_store_root\nASSETS=['BTC','ETH','SOL','XRP','HYPE','XAU','NVDAX','GOOGLX','TSLAX','AAPLX','WTIOIL','SPCX','SP500']\np=argparse.ArgumentParser();p.add_argument('--bin-minutes',type=int,choices=[5,10,15,30],default=30);p.add_argument('--parent');p.add_argument('--parent-weight',type=float,default=.5);p.add_argument('--weekly',action='store_true');p.add_argument('--end',default='2026-09-01');p.add_argument('--start',default='2026-06-01');p.add_argument('--output',default='agent/seasonality.json');a=p.parse_args()\nbins=1440//a.bin_minutes\nparent=json.loads(Path(a.parent).read_text())['assets'] if a.parent else None\nif parent and a.weekly:p.error('Fine-parent shrinkage and weekly refinement must be separate experiments')\nout={'bin_minutes':a.bin_minutes,'parent':a.parent,'parent_weight':a.parent_weight if a.parent else 0.,'train_start':a.start,'train_end_exclusive':a.end,'assets':{}}\nfor asset in ASSETS:\n files=[f for f in sorted(default_store_root(asset).glob('date=*.parquet')) if a.start<=f.stem[5:]<a.end]\n frame=pd.concat([pd.read_parquet(f,columns=['timestamp','close']) for f in files]).set_index('timestamp').sort_index()\n log=np.log(frame.close.astype(float));r=log.diff(5)\n # Gaps are not filled. Both five-minute endpoints must be observed.\n daily=r.pow(2).resample('1D').mean();baseline=daily.rolling(14,min_periods=3,center=True).median()\n norm=baseline.reindex(r.index.floor('D')).to_numpy();z=r.to_numpy()/np.sqrt(norm)\n z=np.clip(z,-8,8)\n local=r.index.tz_convert('America/New_York');bucket=local.hour*(60//a.bin_minutes)+local.minute//a.bin_minutes;weekend=(local.dayofweek>=5).astype(int)\n df=pd.DataFrame({'z':z,'b':bucket,'w':weekend,'dow':local.dayofweek})\n groups=df.groupby(['w','b']).z\n var=groups.apply(lambda x: np.nanmean(x*x)).unstack().reindex(index=[0,1],columns=range(bins))\n absolute=groups.apply(lambda x:np.nanmean(np.abs(x))).unstack().reindex(index=[0,1],columns=range(bins))**2\n var=var/np.nanmean(var.to_numpy()[0]);absolute=absolute/np.nanmean(absolute.to_numpy()[0]);shape=.5*var+.5*absolute\n shape=shape.fillna(1).to_numpy();shape=.9*shape+.1*np.mean(shape[0]);shape/=np.mean(shape[0])\n if parent is not None:\n  prior=np.repeat(np.asarray(parent[asset]),30//a.bin_minutes,axis=1)\n  shape=(1-a.parent_weight)*shape+a.parent_weight*prior\n  shape/=np.mean(shape[0])\n if a.weekly:\n  weekly_var=df.groupby(['dow','b']).z.apply(lambda x:np.nanmean(x*x)).unstack().reindex(index=range(7),columns=range(bins))\n  weekly_abs=df.groupby(['dow','b']).z.apply(lambda x:np.nanmean(np.abs(x))).unstack().reindex(index=range(7),columns=range(bins))**2\n  weekly_var/=np.nanmean(weekly_var.to_numpy()[:5]);weekly_abs/=np.nanmean(weekly_abs.to_numpy()[:5])\n  w=(.5*weekly_var+.5*weekly_abs).fillna(1).to_numpy()\n  base=shape[(np.arange(7)>=5).astype(int)]\n  w=.5*w+.5*base\n  w/=w[:5].mean()\n  out['assets'][asset]=w.tolist()\n else:out['assets'][asset]=shape.tolist()\n print(asset,'weekend/weekday vol',round(float(np.sqrt(np.mean(shape[1])/np.mean(shape[0]))),3),'intraday max/min vol',round(float(np.sqrt(shape[0].max()/shape[0].min())),2),'density',round(float(frame.close.notna().mean()),3),flush=True)\nPath(a.output).write_text(json.dumps(out,indent=2))\n71M\tagent/results\n agent/modeling.py | 6 +++++-\n 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
