{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_308","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'head -30 agent/report.md; tail -30 agent/report.md; cat agent/suggestions.md; git diff --cached --numstat | tail -3'","aggregated_output":"# Calendar-time volatility for Synth price-path simulation\n\nResearch in progress. No final champion has been nominated.\n\n## Context\n\nSynth scores an ensemble of price paths using CRPS: the forecast must express uncertainty at several return horizons, not merely predict the terminal price. The one-hour configuration also scores realized volatility. This campaign uses only the frozen minute-price snapshot through October 11, 2026, and the provided calendar. All reported CRPS comes from the pinned official backtester, at the validator's 1000-path ensemble size and actual prompt times. Earlier one-hour prompts are rescored with the latest volatility-inclusive formula.\n\n## Approach\n\nThe first useful addition is predictable calendar-time variation. Fit weekday/weekend half-hour variance profiles in New York time on June–August prices, before the validation windows. Normalize by a rolling volatility level, combine squared and absolute-return estimates, and shrink the profiles toward a common level. Estimate current volatility from the seven-day context after removing that recurring shape; combine fast and slow exponentially weighted estimates and gradually revert forecasts toward the slower estimate. Restore the known future seasonal shape along each simulated path.\n\nUse one-minute returns for one-hour forecasts and five-minute returns for 24-hour forecasts. NaN observations remain missing: no interpolation, no fabricated prices, and no treating a multi-minute gap as a one-minute return. A robust absolute-return variance blend also improves commodity/equity CRPS incrementally across both full windows; it is rejected for crypto. The seed is read from SYNTH_BENCHMARK_SEED. Path count and length honor the caller's arguments.\n\n## Experiments\n\nThe seeded starter is a constant-volatility lognormal random walk. Shortening volatility memory alone yields small, inconsistent improvements for 24-hour paths. The seasonal model improves all 36 asset/format/window cells in two initial validation windows: two scored days ending September 16 and two ending October 10.\n\nFiltered empirical innovations retain substantial tails after volatility normalization, but are weaker than the selected mixture for one-hour CRPS. Large residual kurtosis alone does not establish a useful forecast change.\n\nA global 15% width reduction failed, as did a finer seven-day seasonal profile and a fitted range/volume residual correction. Shortening the daily fast-memory half-life to three hours also failed (only 6/26 cells improved), although a 15-minute fast half-life improved all ten one-hour cells on the full windows. The residual model improved a future-volatility proxy but reversed sign between September and October on actual CRPS. This distinction matters: predicting volatility more accurately under a surrogate loss does not necessarily improve the submitted distribution.\n\nLognormal path-level volatility uncertainty (log standard deviation 0.35, unit second moment) improves one-hour crypto and commodity/equity forecasts. It worsens crypto 24-hour forecasts, where we retain Gaussian increments. The selected mixture improves all 39 affected stress-test cells. On a BTC/SOL component audit, both official price and volatility CRPS improve, but 76–83% of the absolute gain comes from price CRPS. The much larger percentage improvement in the smaller volatility term should not be mistaken for its contribution to the total. Minute-of-hour profiles add a smaller consistent crypto gain: 19/20 initial cells improve. Sobol sampling has small, seed-dependent effects and is not selected universally.\n\nCalendar kernels use June–August events as independent observations, pooling assets within each release and shrinking estimates. Simultaneous releases combine by maximum rather than multiplication. Prospective tests cover later CPI/PPI, FOMC and payroll windows: 49/54 cells improve, with all nine configuration/window averages favorable. Holiday suppression is not selected. A July–September profile used only after its training cutoff improves all eight commodity/equity assets on the full October window (mean per-asset gain 0.629%). Reducing seasonal shrinkage from 10% to 2% improves 9/10 full-window crypto 24-hour cells; it is retained only for that configuration. Stronger event kernels improve 34/36 full-window cells and all six configuration/window means. They are being checked with the refreshed profiles in a canonical float32 combined run.\n\nA small public pretrained model, Chronos-T5-tiny, forecasts hourly deseasonalized variance from context alone. A quarter-blend improves 9/10 initial one-hour cells but slightly worsens crypto 24-hour results. It also improves 9/10 cells beyond the stronger fast-memory baseline (mean per-asset gains 0.153% and 0.144%). A matched hourly-volatility persistence control worsens both windows. The pretrained model remains experimental until it passes full-window tests with the selected volatility mixture. Offline weights, exact revision, latency and reproducibility checks are recorded in the journal.\n\nHistorical validation uses the official backtester scoring worker on cached validator prompt times. It exactly reproduces ordinary backtest CRPS on three representative parity checks. With profiles fitted only through July1, seasonality improves 33/36 July/August asset-window cells and all six configuration means. These runs make no field-rank claim.\n\nA small public pretrained model, Chronos-T5-tiny, forecasts hourly deseasonalized variance from context alone. A quarter-blend improves 9/10 initial one-hour cells but slightly worsens crypto 24-hour results. It also improves 9/10 cells beyond the stronger fast-memory baseline (mean per-asset gains 0.153% and 0.144%). A matched hourly-volatility persistence control worsens both windows. The pretrained model remains experimental until it passes full-window tests with the selected volatility mixture. Offline weights, exact revision, latency and reproducibility checks are recorded in the journal.\n\nHistorical validation uses the official backtester scoring worker on cached validator prompt times. It exactly reproduces ordinary backtest CRPS on three representative parity checks. With profiles fitted only through July1, seasonality improves 33/36 July/August asset-window cells and all six configuration means. These runs make no field-rank claim.\n\n## Results\n\n![Candidate v1 CRPS reductions at 1000 paths](figures/candidate_v1_full.png)\n\nThese are official CRPS means, averaged equally across assets within each configuration, at 1000 paths. The current provisional candidate v5 combines seasonal EWMA, crypto within-hour profiles, stronger calendar kernels, group-specific volatility mixtures, faster one-hour memory, lower crypto 24-hour seasonal shrinkage, and refreshed robust commodity/equity variance forecasts. It improves all 36 asset/format/window cells in the two ten-day comparisons. Crypto results use v4’s identical behavior; commodity/equity results come from v5’s direct run, all at float32 precision.\n\n| Scored days | Configuration | Starter | Provisional v5 |\n|---|---|---:|---:|\n| Sep09–18 | CRYPTO_24H | 4296.6726 | 4210.5414 |\n| Sep09–18 | COM_EQU_24H | 2316.0261 | 2096.5230 |\n| Sep09–18 | CRYPTO_1H | 960.4266 | 925.0679 |\n| Oct01–10 | CRYPTO_24H | 3531.2042 | 3424.4340 |\n| Oct01–10 | COM_EQU_24H | 2065.4112 | 1803.7327 |\n| Oct01–10 | CRYPTO_1H | 741.2317 | 691.3428 |\n\nFor the earlier v1 checkpoint shown in the figure, paired circular two-day block resampling gives descriptive intervals below zero in all six configuration/window combinations; these intervals do not correct for repeated model selection. That checkpoint improves all 20 daily configuration means, although individual asset-days can regress (HYPE one-hour on Sep17 is one example). Current mean per-asset percentage reductions are 2.17%, 8.92%, 3.96% in September and 3.13%, 12.53%, 6.96% in October, in table configuration order. Ratios of the aggregate means differ because those give greater influence to assets with larger raw CRPS. These are development-inclusive validation windows, not untouched holdouts or the unseen campaign score.\n\nOn the available CPU, the provisional model generated 1000 paths in 0.017 seconds for BTC one-hour and 0.041 seconds for a 24-hour forecast. The sparse AAPLX context tested had 3495 missing close observations and produced valid paths. Small arbitrary ensemble counts and repeatable/changeable seeds also passed contract checks.\n\n## Limitations\n\nValidation includes two ten-day windows, release stress windows, and earlier causal-profile checks, but substantial repeated model selection remains. Adjacent 24-hour prompts overlap substantially; prompt count is not independent sample size. Seasonal profiles may drift as these young perpetual markets mature. Cached validator paths and minute candles differ in missingness and stale prices. Event types have few independent dates, and some release effects cannot be separated. Float32 versus float64 baseline output changes full-window cell means by at most 0.000012%; selected model gains are much larger. Two additional seeds improve all 72 asset/window cells for the earlier v3 checkpoint. No rank improvement is used as evidence of model improvement.\n\n## What more budget is for\n\nFinish incremental checks of faster volatility memory, calendar strength and profile refresh; test filtered empirical innovations; check canonical float32 output precision and multiple seeds of the final combination. These studies remain active. Final selection will follow official paired CRPS and serving reproducibility.\n# Suggestions for the benchmark and future work\n\n- The campaign says BUDGET.md is at the workspace root; this run's harness writes agent/BUDGET.md. Align the documented location.\n- Keep the raw-context issuance cutoff explicit. The supplied evaluator originally took the last 10080 rows before issuance; the official generator uses the seven calendar days ending at issuance. This run aligns with the official generator.\n- Daily NumPy predictions substantially reduce serialization overhead compared with one JSON file per prompt. The official loader already supports them; make this the default in the starter evaluator.\n- Calendar data sometimes co-locates GDP and PCE. Treat their effects jointly instead of estimating independent additive effects from the same release observations.\n- DATA.md omits the measured scored-prompt span described in CAMPAIGN.md. This bundle starts on September 8, 2026 at approximately 23:00 UTC, despite cached realized paths starting in June. Explicitly report both spans.\n- Official backtest raises KeyError('miner_uid') when a one-prompt window leaves no smoothed-score rows. It should return CRPS with an unavailable rank, or raise a coverage error before prediction generation.\n\n- Expose a supported CRPS-only mode using cached validator prompt schedules when field scores are unavailable. This campaign verified exact parity through the internal official scoring worker, but a public API would remove reliance on private internals.\n- Preserve the official scorer's component totals in backtest output. The validator already computes price-interval and volatility details, but the backtester worker discards them. Keeping them would make distribution-model failures easier to diagnose without changing the metric.\n19\t0\tagent/summarize_components.py\n13\t0\tagent/summarize_seeds.py\n19\t0\tagent/verify_garch_context.py\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
