{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_340","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'Sobol|antithetic|sampling' agent/journal.md | head -20; cat agent/results/sobol/config.txt 2>/dev/null; sed -n '203,225p' agent/research_model.py\"","aggregated_output":"79:## Iteration 6 — finite-ensemble sampling (prepared)\n81:Hypothesis: at the mandatory 1000-path size, scrambled Sobol normals or marginally stratified normals reduce sampling error while preserving the same forecast distribution. This is an ensemble-generation experiment, not a new market signal. Sobol's generator receives a seed derived from the environment-seeded NumPy generator; no unseeded randomness is introduced.\n83:Change: optional samplers implemented in research_model. Sobol includes an extra coordinate for path volatility; stratification preserves each base-step Gaussian marginal. Neither is selected or claimed to improve CRPS yet.\n111:Resource scheduling: on this resumed process tree, bounded queues reserve the cores of existing suites instead of oversubscribing. After clock exits: residual proxy fit, fine-event diagnostics/fit, then Sobol suite (2 cores). After stress_events exits: aligned-event suite (4 cores). After stress_seasonal exits: seeded starter baseline on TWO TEN-DAY windows, scored Sep09–18 and Oct01–10 inclusive (4 cores). Current weekly and stress_mixture retain 2 cores each. Maximum remains 14. New baseline_full establishes the final longer-window comparison; it does not use ranks or post-cutoff data. Queues are scripts, not agents; no subagent delegation.\n149:Evaluation durability: write candidate parquet and summary via temporary files and atomic renames, with summary published last. The suite resumes only when a valid JSON summary and readable nonempty parquet footer both exist, preventing an interrupted write from being mistaken for a completed cell. This changes storage only; official scoring is unchanged. Historical experiment arrays are float64 NumPy files (as were the starter JSON floats); the harness's canonical generator writes float32. Before final nomination, repeat the selected model and baseline at canonical float32 precision to bound any effect on small sampling gains.\n153:Hypothesis: separately validated seasonal, within-hour, event and group-specific mixture effects survive combination. Candidate v1 combines original seasonal EWMA, crypto within-hour shape, five-minute event kernels, and mixture0.35 for crypto1h and com/equ24h only. No unvalidated holiday suppression, residual ML, refresh, stronger mixture, finer kernel, or sampling change is included.\n185:Iteration 6 multi-seed result: on BTC/SOL/AAPLX/SP500 across three seeds and two windows, Sobol mean change is -0.0700% com/equ24h, -0.0164% crypto24h, but +0.0235% crypto1h. Results vary by seed, and crypto24h reverses at seed37. Verdict: do not promote a universal Sobol sampler. Test marginal stratification separately: it exactly balances each base-step Gaussian marginal at N=1000, unlike truncating a 1024-point Sobol net. Retain the original sampling pre-registered multi-seed requirement for any promotion.\n196:Mean per-asset percentage reductions: September 2.0006%,8.7439%,3.7744%; October 3.0694%,11.8400%,6.9060%. Every configuration's daily mean improves on all 20 scored days, but some asset-days regress (notably HYPE1h Sep17, approximately +14%). Verdict: KEEP and promote to provisional modeling.py; preserve seasonal_checkpoint.py. No CHAMPION yet: faster memory, historical checks, calibration and sampling work remain worthwhile within the envelope.\n220:Hypothesis: standardized observed returns retain tails and central concentration beyond Gaussian increments, potentially improving the one-hour realized-volatility distribution. Before implementing bootstrap, measure June–August returns divided by a strictly lagged seasonal EWMA scale: kurtosis, tail probabilities, and central absolute quantiles. Compare minute and five-minute samples. These diagnostics are not CRPS and cannot promote an innovation model. If excess tails survive filtering, test symmetric empirical resampling with unit variance; preserve zero drift, same volatility forecast and official 1000-path scoring. Reject if gains fail either window; do not assume a large kurtosis implies useful CRPS improvement.\n238:## Iteration 20 — paired antithetic sampling with volatility mixtures\n240:Hypothesis: pairing each complete innovation vector with its negative reduces finite-ensemble location error. For a volatility mixture, each pair must share the same volatility draw; otherwise the intended symmetry is lost. Implement this pairing only behind a research option, preserving Gaussian/mixture distributions and environment-derived seeds. Compare candidate v1 against paired antithetic on BTC/SOL/AAPLX/SP500, both formats where applicable, two initial windows, three seeds (20261011,11,37). This is a sampling change, not extra market information. Require favorable pooled mean in each configuration and consistency across seeds before promotion; report any one-hour volatility-score tradeoff. Do not select based on one seed.\n256:Uncertainty reporting plan: summarize v1 versus baseline with paired asset-day CRPS differences and circular two-day block resampling inside each ten-day window. Resample days jointly across assets to retain shared shocks. This is a descriptive interval on development-selected observations, not a confirmatory significance test; overlapping24h horizons and model selection remain limitations.\n298:Iteration20 three-seed result: paired antithetic pooled mean changes are COM-0.0384%, crypto24h+0.0202%, crypto1h+0.0582%. Crypto24h flips from a default-seed gain to losses under11/37; one-hour worsens on every seed's pooled mean. DISCARD universal antithetic sampling. COM-only effect is small and cross-asset inconsistent (AAPLX/SP500); retain random sampling pending broader evidence rather than selecting a tiny pooled improvement from two assets.\n352:## Iteration31 — Sobol under the selected COM mixture\n354:Hypothesis: the earlier small Sobol gain on two Gaussian COM assets survives the selected volatility mixture and all8 assets. Compare v3 COM at1000 paths over both initial windows and seeds20261011/11/37; existing seed11/37 v3 runs supply controls, and default-seed COM controls are generated explicitly. Require all three pooled seed means to improve and predominantly favorable asset/window cells before any serving promotion. Crypto sampling stays unchanged. A favorable two-asset result is not sufficient evidence for the full group.\n356:Iteration24 full combined result: latent-volatility reversion improves15/16 COM cells at float32 versus v5, mean per-asset gains0.0270%/0.0341%; CRPS2096.5230→2095.9403 and1803.7327→1803.1622. KEEP as a candidate, but defer serving promotion until independent-seed checks. Its effect is small enough that a shared random fan can create apparently consistent asset/window signs. Test the identical reversion on v3 COM at three seeds using the controls already generated for the Sobol audit; this isolates the stochastic-law change without paying for duplicate controls. Full v5 validation above measures interaction with robust variance.\n374:Iteration31 three-seed COM result: Sobol improves all six seed/window means, all three pooled seed means (0.0475%,0.0483%,0.0567%), and33/48 cells; pooled gain0.0508%. KEEP COM-only Sobol for full combined-model validation. Crypto sampling remains rejected.\n376:Iteration24 seed audit: reversion improves all three pooled seed means (0.0136%,0.0060%,0.0119%),30/48 cells, pooled0.0105%. One seed/window slightly regresses (+0.0027%). This is weaker than the default-seed full/stress evidence; keep as a candidate, but require a benefit on top of Sobol before adding both stochastic refinements.\n380:Fit fine Sep14/Oct05 tables with the same ten-minute/50%-parent rule. Candidate v6 combines those dated fine tables with the validated v5, without sampling or latent-reversion changes. At1000 paths/float32, compare both full COM windows against fine_v5 (incremental refresh) and rolling_v5 (incremental resolution). Require favorable means in both windows and most cells before promoting the combination. Other configurations are unchanged.\n382:## Iteration34 — sampling/reversion interaction\n{'config': '{\"seasonal\":true,\"sampler\":\"sobol\"}', 'tag': 'sobol', 'model': 'research_model', 'ends': ['2026-09-16', '2026-10-10'], 'days': 2, 'workers': 2, 'assets': ['BTC', 'ETH', 'SOL', 'XRP', 'HYPE', 'XAU', 'NVDAX', 'GOOGLX', 'TSLAX', 'AAPLX', 'WTIOIL', 'SPCX', 'SP500'], 'formats': ['24h', '1h'], 'paths': 1000}    n=num_simulations\n    mixture_normal=None\n    if c.get('sampler')=='sobol':\n        from scipy.stats import qmc\n        from scipy.special import ndtri\n        engine=qmc.Sobol(d=steps+1,scramble=True,seed=int(rng.integers(0,2**32-1)))\n        u=engine.random_base2(int(np.ceil(np.log2(max(n,1)))))[:n]\n        normal=ndtri(np.clip(u,1e-12,1-1e-12))\n        z=normal[:,:steps];mixture_normal=normal[:,-1:]\n    elif c.get('sampler')=='stratified':\n        from scipy.special import ndtri\n        ranks=rng.uniform(size=(n,steps)).argsort(axis=0)\n        z=ndtri((ranks+.5)/n)\n    elif c.get('antithetic',False) or c.get('paired',False):\n        z=rng.normal(size=((n+1)//2,steps));z=np.concatenate([z,-z])[:n]\n    else:z=rng.normal(size=(n,steps))\n    if c.get('empirical',False):\n        from scipy.special import ndtr\n        quantiles=np.asarray(table('innovations.json')[asset][str(lag)])\n        u=2*np.abs(ndtr(z)-.5)\n        z=np.sign(z)*np.interp(u,(np.arange(len(quantiles))+.5)/len(quantiles),quantiles)\n    df=c.get('df',0)\n    if df:z=z/np.sqrt(rng.chisquare(df,size=z.shape)/df)*np.sqrt((df-2)/df)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
